text:
In which we bring you motoring news from around the Web:
• Ron Burgundy, the fictional 1970s television news anchorman, will be returning to theaters in “Anchorman 2″ this December. In the meantime, the character, played by Will Ferrell, has appeared in a number of YouTube advertisements for the new Dodge Durango. In typical Burgundy style, the ads are a mixture of farcical ridicule and loud talking, and they are hilarious. So far, four spots have been released, in eight different versions. A total of 70 videos are planned for the coming months. (
Ad Week
)
• The Harley-Davidson motorcycle given to Pope Francis by Harley aficionados who had rallied to the Vatican over the summer is headed for the auction block. The pontiff, who has been vocal about his rejection of flashy possessions, had spoken to the bikers assembled at St. Peter’s Square in June about the dangers of “fleeting human idols which offer the intoxication of a flash of freedom, but in the end bring new forms of slavery and death,” and will be selling his Hog to benefit a charity hostel and soup kitchen near Rome’s Termini Station.
(
The Telegraph
)
• According to a report from Automotive News, demand for BMW’s i3 electric vehicle has exceeded expectation. The automaker is considering a production increase for the E.V., the report says, after receiving more than 8,000 reservations before it becomes available in Europe in November. Friedrich Eichiner, BMW’s chief financial officer, said in a press conference Monday that the company expected it would sell more than 10,000 i3s in 2014 and will adjust production to meet customer demand. (
Automotive News
)
• Rental car customers aren’t going for electric vehicles, says a report from Bloomberg. Car rental companies. like Enterprise and Hertz, which had planned to have substantial E.V. fleets, have not been able to increase electric numbers because of low customer demand. Lee Broughton, who heads Enterprise’s sustainability program, said that many customers brought E.V.’s back after renting them to trade for something gasoline-powered. (
Bloomberg
)
• General Motors has increased the price of its full-size pickup trucks by about 6 percent, according to a report from Reuters. G.M. said it would offset the roughly $1,500 price increase with a $1,500 cash back incentive through the end of October. According to Reuters, big pickups are G.M.’s cash cow, generating $12,000 profit per unit. (
Reuters
)
• The “Ride for the Constitution” – a trucker convoy formed to protest against excessive government intrusion – turned out not to be a hoax. Only about 30 truckers showed up for the protest on Friday, and authorities reported that nothing out of the ordinary occurred. Although protest organizers had promised to snarl traffic, it didn’t seem to be any worse than usual, reported The Washington Post. (
The Washington Post
)
